  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24–48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The October 3 Camp David session brought Vice President Vance, Secretary of State Rubio, Defense Secretary Hegseth, White House Envoy Witkoff, CIA Director Ratcliffe, and Joint Chiefs Chairman Caine together for several unannounced hours on Iran and Yemen. Axios broke the story; the White House issued no readout. At least one participant told reporters that decisions had been reached. The session mirrors the Camp David convening held in June 2025, days before the US-Israeli air campaign against Iran commenced. Trump told reporters afterward that a decision on Iran was &#8220;coming imminently&#8221; and offered the choice as &#8220;the easy way or the hard way.&#8221; → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>Three commercial vessels were struck near the Strait of Hormuz on October 4–5: an LPG tanker struck at 1716 UTC on October 4, a crude oil tanker at 1907 UTC the same day, and a third vessel on October 5. UK Maritime Trade Operations logged five separate incidents across that period. As of October 6, 242 AIS-visible vessels held position outside the Gulf watch box. Commercial passage through the strait ran at near-zero throughout September and early October — only one transit recorded September 27 — and the 30-vessel-per-day rolling average threshold was never approached before the October 3 resolution date. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Iranian President Pezeshkian, speaking October 5, declared negotiations with the United States &#8220;meaningless,&#8221; citing three episodes of resumed US military action following diplomatic contacts. Iran&#8217;s parliament national security committee spokesman stated the strait would remain closed until Washington met seven conditions — lifting the naval blockade, releasing frozen assets, and ending all active fronts among them. The position closed off the September 23 UNGA road map in which Tehran had proposed a seven-day phased Hormuz reopening and a 60-day regional ceasefire. Qatari-mediated contacts on September 28–29 produced no agreed text. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>UN Secretary General Guterres arrived in Islamabad on October 6 for a three-day official visit; meetings with President Zardari, Prime Minister Sharif, Deputy Prime Minister Dar, and Army Chief Munir placed Iran-US mediation on the agenda. Pakistan has served as a third channel alongside Qatar and Egypt since the June Islamabad MoU. No deliverable was pre-announced. The last confirmed Iran-US indirect session — Qatari envoys shuttling for three hours between Witkoff and Araghchi at UNGA on September 22 — remained the sole confirmed session in the post-MoU period through October 6. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, supports <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>Three polls from the closing ten days of September showed Gadi Eisenkot&#8217;s Yashar party leading Likud by three to five seats in individual surveys; an i24NEWS outlier placed Likud at 27 versus Yashar at 22. The five-poll average put Yashar at 23.6 seats and Likud at 21.8. Neither the right-religious bloc nor the opposition bloc held a governing majority in any survey, each hovering near 52–54 seats out of 120. The October 27 election date stands. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span>, supports <span class="cite">F3</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

