  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media gave prominent placement on October 5–6 to Pezeshkian&#8217;s declaration that US-Iran negotiations were &#8220;meaningless,&#8221; framing the statement as a principled defense of national sovereignty rather than a diplomatic retreat. Press TV and IRNA reproduced the president&#8217;s claim that Washington had attacked Iran three times after diplomatic contacts, positioning the United States as a party that uses negotiations as cover for continued military pressure. Tasnim ran extended commentary arguing that Iran&#8217;s seven enumerated conditions for reopening the Strait of Hormuz constituted minimum national-interest requirements, not an opening bid. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Coverage of IRGC interdiction actions against tankers on October 4–5 framed the strikes not as escalation but as lawful enforcement of maritime policy established since late February, noting vessels had received repeated warnings. The Guterres Pakistan visit received minimal domestic airtime; state media treated it as peripheral rather than a meaningful diplomatic channel. An ongoing tension between the Pezeshkian government — which has pressed the case for negotiated solutions — and IRIB&#8217;s pro-hardline editorial orientation continued to shape domestic narration of diplomatic developments; the government information council accused IRIB of selective coverage of the president&#8217;s speeches. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

